					<span class="printuser avatarhover"><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/thekillerax" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(1832151); return false;"  ><img class="small" src="http://www.wikidot.com/avatar.php?userid=1832151&amp;amp;size=small&amp;amp;timestamp=1607958888" alt="Thekillerax" style="background-image:url(http://www.wikidot.com/userkarma.php?u=1832151)"/></a><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/thekillerax" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(1832151); return false;" >Thekillerax</a></span> <span class="odate time_1410841571 format_%25e%20%25b%20%25Y%2C%20%25H%3A%25M%7Cagohover">16 Sep 2014 04:26</span>
														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-2110765">
									

<p>Beautifully tragic and moving. As someone who has never connected with nature, I could still picture myself in the artist's shoes and envision their desires. I was bracing myself for the hamfisted finale of &quot;Foundation burns tree down hurrdurr tragedy for the sake of tragedy&quot;, but was pleasantly surprised by the bittersweet ending written in. Especially captivating was the fact that the Foundation didn't even know what it had done and yet still managed to finally help the world on a small scale.<br />
Splendid work, Zyn</p>

							</div>

										<div class="signature">
					<hr class="signature-separator"/>
					

<p>And if the whole world is crashing down&#8230; fall through space out of mind with me.</p>

				</div>
					
					
