				<div class="info">
					<span class="printuser avatarhover"><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/ensimismada" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(1451590); return false;"  ><img class="small" src="http://www.wikidot.com/avatar.php?userid=1451590&amp;amp;size=small&amp;amp;timestamp=1607958888" alt="ensimismada" style="background-image:url(http://www.wikidot.com/userkarma.php?u=1451590)"/></a><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/ensimismada" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(1451590); return false;" >ensimismada</a></span> <span class="odate time_1411187161 format_%25e%20%25b%20%25Y%2C%20%25H%3A%25M%7Cagohover">20 Sep 2014 04:26</span>
														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-2114030">
									

<p>Despite the high risk of being overly picky, I'll point out that it's very hard to get DNA from hair, especially nuclear DNA, the stuff with the most information. Way more information than you probably want to know here: <a href="http://www.forensicmag.com/articles/2013/04/challenges-dna-testing-and-forensic-analysis-hair-samples">http://www.forensicmag.com/articles/2013/04/challenges-dna-testing-and-forensic-analysis-hair-samples</a></p>
<p>Also, I fixed a little grammar nitpick/possible typo.</p>
<p>As for the meat of the story, I liked it. I'm a nature person and I completely understand the feeling invoke at the beginning, where you see/experience a scene in nature so heart-stirringly beautiful that you just want it to last and last and it makes you stop to try and soak it in permanently. Those moments indeed feel like masterpieces.</p>
<p>The good Doctor's treatment of the AO feels a bit &quot;off&quot; for Foundation standards, but in a quiet, reflective, peaceful sort of way. These are not feelings I often get reading SCP tales, so +1 for pulling it off so nicely.</p>

							</div>

								
